{
  "analysis": "I'm at a fresh shell in /app. The task requires downloading a YouTube video of Zork gameplay, performing OCR to read the on-screen text, and extracting the player's typed commands into /app/solution.txt. First I need to check what tools are available (yt-dlp, ffmpeg, tesseract, etc.).",
  "plan": "First, check available tools: yt-dlp/youtube-dl for download, ffmpeg for frame extraction, and tesseract for OCR. Then download the video, extract frames, run OCR, and parse the commands. Let me start by probing the environment.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which yt-dlp youtube-dl ffmpeg tesseract python3 pip3 2>&1; echo '---'; python3 --version\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Only python3 is available. No yt-dlp, youtube-dl, ffmpeg, tesseract, or pip3. I need to install these tools. Let me check package manager availability and internet connectivity.",
  "plan": "Check for apt-get and pip, and test internet connectivity. Then install yt-dlp (via pip), ffmpeg (via apt), and tesseract (via apt). I'll start by checking apt and pip availability.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which apt-get pip 2>&1; echo '---'; pip3 --version 2>&1; echo '---'; cat /etc/os-release 2>&1 | head -3\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Ubuntu 24.04 with apt-get available, but no pip, no yt-dlp, ffmpeg, or tesseract. Need to install all of these. Let me first verify internet connectivity, then install tools.",
  "plan": "Run apt-get update, then install ffmpeg, tesseract-ocr, and python3-pip. Install yt-dlp via pip. First test connectivity with a quick update.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get update 2>&1 | tail -5\n",
      "duration": 30
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "apt-get update succeeded, confirming internet connectivity. Now I'll install the needed packages: ffmpeg (frame extraction), tesseract-ocr (OCR), and python3-pip (to install yt-dlp).",
  "plan": "Install ffmpeg, tesseract-ocr, and python3-pip in one apt-get install command. This may take a while, so I'll give it a generous duration then poll.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "apt-get install -y ffmpeg tesseract-ocr python3-pip 2>&1 | tail -15\n",
      "duration": 60
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The apt-get install failed due to insufficient disk space in /var/cache/apt/archives/. Need to check available disk space and clean up to free room before installing.",
  "plan": "Check disk space with df -h. If low, clean apt cache and any unnecessary files. Then retry the install.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h / /tmp /var 2>&1; echo '---'; du -sh /var/cache/apt/archives 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The root filesystem is at 100% capacity (960G used, 0 available). The apt cache is nearly empty, so the space is consumed elsewhere. I need to identify and remove large unnecessary files to free up space for the install.",
  "plan": "Find what's consuming disk space. Check common culprits like /tmp, log files, and large directories. I'll use du to find large directories under / (excluding virtual filesystems).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /tmp /var/log /root /home /usr 2>/dev/null; echo '==='; find / -xdev -type f -size +100M 2>/dev/null | head -20\n",
      "duration": 5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Disk reports 100% full (960G used, 0 avail) but du shows very little actual file usage (~123M in /usr, nothing large). This strongly suggests the 960G is the immutable lower/base layer of the fuse-overlayfs, while our writable upper layer is tiny or also constrained. I need to verify whether I can actually write files. Let me investigate the mount and test writing.",
  "plan": "Check mount details for the overlay upperdir, list top-level directory sizes, and test whether writing a file actually succeeds. This determines whether I can proceed with installing heavy tools or must find a lighter approach.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mount 2>/dev/null | grep -i overlay; echo '---'; cat /proc/mounts | grep -i overlay; echo '---test write---'; dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/testfile bs=1M count=50 2>&1; df -h / | tail -1\n",
      "duration": 2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Interesting: df reports 0 available on / but I successfully wrote a 50MB file to /tmp. The 960G 'used' is the read-only lower layer; the space-reporting is misleading due to fuse-overlayfs. apt refused at its own statfs space check, not an actual write failure. I need to find the real backing disk capacity and work around apt's space check. Let me investigate the backing store and clean up.",
  "plan": "Remove the test file, check the actual backing device capacity (where the overlay upperdir lives in /data), and check docker storage usage. If there's real space, I'll work around apt's size check by either pointing the cache elsewhere or using a trick. First gather info.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "rm -f /tmp/testfile; df -h /data 2>&1; echo '---'; du -sh /data/docker 2>/dev/null; echo '---upper---'; ls -ld /data/docker/lib/overlay2/2d1d09b3abeeafaccb876cf1d0466adaa6c7bd9d8983e1f5f230cbab01f190a4/diff 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The /data overlay paths don't exist inside the container (they're host-side). The / is fuse-overlayfs and df reports 0 avail, yet I successfully wrote 50MB to /tmp — so the disk-space report is misleading (likely reflecting the read-only lower layer). I need to understand the real writable space before fighting apt's disk check. Let me inspect mounts and tmpfs, then decide on a strategy (likely: get yt-dlp as a standalone script, and install ffmpeg/tesseract somehow).",
  "plan": "Check mount types (is /tmp tmpfs?), df on key dirs, and whether apt's disk check can be bypassed by setting the cache dir to a tmpfs. I'll also try a tiny real install to observe apt's actual behavior. Based on results I'll choose: (a) apt with cache on tmpfs, or (b) manual .deb download + dpkg, or (c) pure-python alternatives.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mount | grep -E 'tmpfs|overlay| / ' | head; echo '=== df ==='; df -h /tmp /run /var / 2>&1; echo '=== apt test ==='; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends hello 2>&1 | tail -8\n",
      "duration": 8
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: writing to the filesystem actually works (I successfully wrote 50MB to /tmp), but `df` and apt's statfs report 0 available because the fuse-overlayfs reports the read-only lower layer's space. The only blocker is apt's disk-space pre-check on /var/cache/apt/archives. The fix: mount a tmpfs over that cache dir so statfs reports real free space, letting apt pass its check while actual writes succeed. I'll verify the mount works.",
  "plan": "1. Mount a 2G tmpfs on /var/cache/apt/archives so apt's space check passes. 2. Verify with df. 3. Then retry installing ffmpeg, tesseract-ocr, and python3-pip.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mount -t tmpfs -o size=2g tmpfs /var/cache/apt/archives 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"; df -h /var/cache/apt/archives\n",
      "duration": 1
    }
  ]
}

